WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
When you silence a gene and the cell breaks, did losing it really cause the defect?

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.340
Maybe not: a silencing tool can hit the wrong target.

3
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:14.320
A rescue experiment tests cause by putting a working copy back.

4
00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:19.417
Normally, a gene makes messenger RNA to build a protein.

5
00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:24.577
A silencing guide, an shRNA, pairs with that message.

6
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:28.017
The protein vanishes, and the cell breaks.

7
00:00:29.297 --> 00:00:33.577
If losing it caused the defect, adding a working copy back should cure it.

8
00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:37.277
If an off-target hit broke it, the defect stays.

9
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:45.440
There is a catch. The shRNA is still inside the cell, ready to silence any matching

10
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.860
message. So predict.

11
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.760
How can a rescue copy make the exact same protein and still escape that guide?

12
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:04.513
Change the codons at the target site to synonymous codons, different codes for the

13
00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:10.873
same amino acid. These silent mutations alter the message, so the guide no longer

14
00:01:10.873 --> 00:01:15.193
pairs with it. Yet the amino-acid sequence stays the same.

15
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:18.733
The protein returns, and the cell recovers.

16
00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:23.097
Still, a rescue can mislead if levels are wrong.

17
00:01:24.397 --> 00:01:29.297
A failed rescue means little unless the protein is folded, placed, and near normal

18
00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:34.057
levels. Far too much protein can cause effects of its own.

19
00:01:35.777 --> 00:01:39.777
Researchers check levels with an immunoblot, an antibody test for the protein.

20
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.390
Even a working rescue is not proof alone.

21
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:48.290
Extra copies of one gene can suppress a defect in another.

22
00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.570
Overexpressed APG1, for example, rescues an apg13 mutant.

23
00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:01.250
Sequencing the mutant allele, or remaking the mutation in a clean strain, rules out

24
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.250
a second hit.

25
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:07.003
Used carefully, rescue reveals shared biology across species.

26
00:02:08.403 --> 00:02:15.023
In 1982, David Beach, Barbara Durkacz, and Paul Nurse rescued a fission-yeast cdc2

27
00:02:15.023 --> 00:02:22.363
mutant. They added budding-yeast DNA carrying CDC28: two distant yeasts, one job.

28
00:02:23.663 --> 00:02:27.363
In 1987, a human gene rescued that same mutant.

29
00:02:28.517 --> 00:02:34.697
In 2015, Aashiq Kachroo, Edward Marcotte, and colleagues tested matching human genes

30
00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:36.117
in place of yeast genes.

31
00:02:37.397 --> 00:02:44.177
Of 414 genes yeast cannot live without, 176 restored growth in their assays.

32
00:02:45.597 --> 00:02:53.997
Dividing 176 by 414 gives about 43%, percent, or 47% percent with earlier cases.

33
00:02:54.957 --> 00:02:59.517
Failed swaps do not prove lost function, since failure has several causes.

34
00:03:00.683 --> 00:03:03.303
Rescue can also show where a signal acts.

35
00:03:04.623 --> 00:03:10.503
In 1985, Kathryn Anderson and colleagues studied fly embryos from Toll mutant

36
00:03:10.503 --> 00:03:16.163
mothers. Those dorsalized embryos lack ventral, belly-side structures.

37
00:03:17.603 --> 00:03:22.783
Injected wild-type cytoplasm made the ventralmost part of the pattern form wherever

38
00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:24.063
the needle went in.

39
00:03:25.083 --> 00:03:28.343
Done carefully, putting the missing piece back tests true cause.
